Date: Sun, 17 Jan 93 17:39:11 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: Perry's Thoughtcrime

(This is not a flame. This is not even a mildly angry note. I'm just
puzzled by some points raised by Harry Shapiro's ruling on the Singh
v. Metzger case and would like to hear comments from others. I have no
axe to grind--though I cannote back this assertion up with
references, so I am exposing myself already to warnings and censure!)

Harry Shapiro rules on the Singh v. Metzger case:

(quoting Perry Metzger):

> Paragraph 1
> > Go and do what you want. However, given that you are a barely tolerated
> > person on the list, don't expect any of us to pay you any attention or
> > participate in any of your ventures. 

Perry was quite clearly expressing an opinion. He didn't couch his
statement in misleading or pseudolegalistic bombast, just plain
opinion. (It is true that the didn't say "In my opinion.." or the
asinine "IMHO," but it was crystal clear that these were Perry's
views. IMHO, I think, we all (mostly) do this kind of thing all the
time, in my opinion, of course.)

So how can Perry get a warning and censure for this? He may not have
been especially polite to Mr. Singh, but that's different from:

"1) Not stating sources for his facts"

and 

"2) Not differing between fact and theory"


How many of us cite ironclad, indisputable "sources" for our many
assertions? How many of us are running afoul of these laws by not
footnoting each and every posting? And even with footnotes, who
decides we've adequately "proved" our case?

If someone says AI just won't work, should we haul them up on charges
if they can't "prove" this statement/opinion/speculation?

If the Asatru-Extropians and "Archaic Revival" shaman-Extropians (no
comment!) make outrageous claims, do we censure them? (Have I already
doomed myself merely be using the adjective "outrageous"? After all,
have I _proved_ to everyone's satisfaction that their claims are in
fact usually "outrageous"?)

The best penalty for someone--like Mr.Singh--who continuously makes
outrageously implausible claims is to see his reputation suffer, not
to haul him up on charges. Or to haul up on charges those who point
out the foolishness of their views.

(There's a valid concern that the list not degenerate into
name-calling. But not "stating sources" does not seem to be the main
issue in these cases.)

If Perry can be censured for his comment quoted above, why was Mr.
Singh not also charged with making statements about flying yogis and
bilocality over these past several months? I can't recall him citing
sources for these facts, nor can I recall him distinguishing between
fact and theory.

Granted, Mr. Singh also received some kind of censure as well
(warnings), but not along these lines.

Really, I'm not angry about this, nor am I attacking our esteemed
list manager...it's a hard job, and Harry's doing it well.

I just think this kind of "what is truth" basis for punishment is
going too far. Next thing you know, the humanist/communitarians will
be charging the PPL/anarchocapitalists with being unable to "prove" our
many assertions! (Turnabout is fair play!)

We'll all have to hire net.lawyers.


-Tim

