Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 01:44:25 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: Re: META: Carol's Two Questions

 >We will have to get into the nitty-gritty details of exactly
 >what kinds of functional systems can be conscious, rather than ascribing
 >a mystical universal consciousness to all matter.

 >Hal Finney

Let's say we wanted to build a system / machine with a consciousness similar to
our own. IMO, these are some characteristics required:- 


   (a) Self-awareness. The machine requires a map of the world which
       incorporates itself. It has to understand the concept of 'I',
       from first-hand experience.

   (b) Sensors. It receives visual, auditory, olfactory and tactile inputs from
       its environment.

   (c) Sentience. It must have 'feelings', and be aware of its own internal
       states.

   (d) Intelligence. Provide a massive parallel processing capacity with some
       2 million transputer modules, each module consisting of over 10,000
       'gates'.

   (e) Learning capability. Give it 'plasticity'. The 'gates' are not actually
       logic gates but neural nets, each with up to tens of thousands of inputs
       and outputs.

   (f) Virtual serial processing. Whenever a module fires, it inhibits adjacent
       modules. Modules further apart are inhibited but to a lesser degree -
       results in one dominant module at any given instant.

   (g) Memory is arranged in a similar way to a hypertext system. Each element
       has associations (which have been learned) with several other concepts,
       and these have associations with others in their turn.  

   (h) Include in its memory a 'life-history' of events that were supposed to
       have happened in its past. This provides still more associations and
       meaning to concepts.

   (i) The resulting subjective mental experience is unidirectional, being
       aligned with the thermodynamic arrow of time, and so makes time appear
       irreversible. (I can't see it happening another way.)


Some of these are only needed for the de luxe model; you could remove the
sense of smell for instance, and the machine would still be conscious. And 
processing speed and memory capacity could be greatly reduced. 
 
The most important sense is the optical one. The machine should be able to look
at a roomful of objects, and instantly recognise one of the many objects it has
learned. It can choose to focus attention on a particular object, e.g. a TV set,
and dwell on various associations such as E.M. waves, red leds, plastic case,
designed by Japanese, the BBC etc. It might recall that the previous set that it
owned was a Grundig, which survived for 8 years. But simultaneously, it will be
aware of a large number of objects in the periphery of its visual field, and
will know what each of these objects actually is. As the machine concentrates on
the fact that the TV set has a red led lit when in the standby mode, it will 
know that a pair of loudspeakers are positioned a few feet either side of it,
and that these couldn't possibly be cupboards.
      
It seems reasonable to imagine a machine having this sort of consciousness - 
just a case of having enough processing power and learned experiences. It has
(b), (d), (e), (g) and (h) above. (f) allows it to simulate a serial processor
existing atop parallel hardware giving rise to, as Daniel Dennett says, the
serial 'one-thing-after-another' consciousness stream. 

(a) isn't too difficult to test for. Let it know that it's in its own interest
for it to try to survive and prosper (an intelligent machine, as opposed to
animals with genetically controlled brain wiring, ought to be able to work this
out itself). Then try to exploit it, and watch the reaction.

I think (c) would be the hardest characteristic to incorporate in the machine.
To an extent, you could test for the machine's feeling of pain by observing its
behaviour at the appropriate time. But isn't pain something more than just a
type of non-maskable interrupt that slows or prevents normal processing? How
could we make the machine chuckle to itself (spontaneously) whenever it thinks
about those few Copenhagen Interpretation supporters who imagine that
Schrodinger's Cat is too stupid to know whether it is alive or dead, and only
the human observer is 'clever enough to collapse a wavefunction'?  8-) Or when
it conceives of a motorist who insists on launching his car from a ramp at
90 mph to over(above?)take a few cars and lands in a space in the traffic
stream. The cops suggest that he'd be better off using a genuine flying car,
and turn away, but our friend then protests vociferously.    

Replicate the function of the limbic system, I suppose, with a hippocampus, 
hypothalamus, medio-dorsal thalamus, temporal pole, gyrus hippocampi, septum,
fornix, olfactory bulb, piriform cortex, entorhinal cortex, amygdala, cingulate
gyrus etc and wire them together correctly. As long as everything is functioning
properly this should give us (c) above.

But next we have to decide the absolute minimum needed for consciousness. A
thermostat is 'conscious' of whether the temperature is higher than some 
predetermined point on the scale, but if you plunged it in boiling oil and then
tipped it into liquid nitrogen I don't think it would suffer one tiny bit.
Although you could just leave out sentience, have all the other features and get
a self-organising entity.

Supposing we wanted to explain how an electron could be conscious. For
simplicity, we'll imagine it as a billiard ball - on its surface are photons
arranged in a particular pattern of clusters. It has to be able to recognise
other particles in case it has to interact with them. If I was to observe a
mad axeman approaching me, a particular structure in my brain would fire - the
specific configuration of neurons, or meme - that represents 'axeman'. Or if I
saw a pile of books, the 'book' and 'pile' memes would fire together with other
associated characteristics such as size and colour. 

So the electron has different photon clusters to represent different particles,
and these light up at the appropriate times. No, I think I'll ditch this idea.

A clever electron could do some rather interesting tricks travelling through
time and space. Say it wants to travel from A to B. It somehow 'looks at' all
the possible routes, computes their probability amplitude, then just before
setting off on the first route sets a counter to the p.a. for that route,
travels to B, decrements the counter, if not equal to zero goes backwards in
time to A then forwards back to B, if equal to zero checks to see whether it's
covered every route, if not backwards to A, sets the counter to the p.a. for the
next route and carries on. And the electron can do all the calculations in a
very short time indeed. At the end of a day's work it goes home to rest, but 
has to get up before it left work the previous day.

Well, I don't think an electron could ever be conscious, but there'll be room
for debate on exactly where consciousness begins, for thousands of years yet.

sjw
