Date: Sun, 17 Jan 1993 18:30:32 -0800
From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: Re:  PPL: Why Not Let Convicts Run the Police?

> I agree that under a libertarian society the only legitimate legal
> system would be restitution. My problem lies in "restoring the
> balance."  For robbery, restitution is easy. But what about murder? > How
does someone "restore the balance" when the victim is dead?
> Also, what happens if the criminal can't afford restitution?
 
> Sameer Parekh
 
Excellent questions.  In the case of murder, full restitution is impossible.
 Partial restitution (e.g. payment to next of kin) is the closest we can
adhere to the restitution principle; we have to acknowledge that restitution
is incomplete.
 
If the convicted criminal can't afford full restitution, again restitution
is incomplete.  Same problem.
 
I see several options.  The defendant can make payments to the plaintiff
until his obligation is discharged.  If the defendant is unwilling or unable
to do this, the plaintiff can drop the matter.  Alternatively, the plaintiff
can "punish" the defendant (cause loss to him with no corresponding benefit
to himself), subtracting that loss from the award that would otherwise be
due him.  
 
Personally, my priorities would be 1) The defendant keeps paying me
voluntarily.  2) I seize his assets as, and when, I can find them.  3) I
take revenge.  (Not being a Christian, I don't turn the other cheek.)
 
Getting back to murder:  Stipulate that someone murders your spouse.  
After the murderer's apprehension and conviction, you murder him.  Can his
estate, for example, his spouse, successfully sue you for damages?
 
Yes.  The harm done to her is then subtracted from the harm done to you.
You would owe her the difference, presumably zero.
 
Bear in mind, if the wrong man is convicted and this fact is later brought
to light, you will then owe restitution to him.  Protecting yourself from
this liability is one strong reason not inflict capital punishment.  
 
Incidentally, for robbery, restitution is NOT necessarily easy.  Restoring
the money is one thing.  Additional damages may include psychological
trauma, particularly if we're speaking of armed robbery.
 
Does a Libertarian legal system (necessarily based on restitution) provide
adequate disincentive to criminals?  One condition of living in a particular
private community could be the requirement of contractual agreement that
conviction of a crime results in punitive as well as actual damages.  The
punitive damages could, for example, be assigned to the community to fund
assistance to uncompensated crime victims.
 
Someone who didn't sign the agreement, who gained access to this private
community by trespass, can be assumed to have given implicit consent to
abide by the terms of that contract.  (Everybody knows the rules here.  You
broke in and now pretend you didn't know?  We think you did and we're
holding you to it.)

