From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: Re:  PPL: Why Not Let Convicts Run the Police?

> In the modern society, with no barbarians to send a bad guy to, we 
> have to put them in jail -- for the purpose of separating them from 
> the society, so that they will do no harm...
 
> Krzys'
 
One of the major advantages of private communities is that criminals CAN be
exiled--prison is not the only way to separate them from society.  The only
legitimate purpose of incarceration is to force unwilling convicted
criminals to complete the restitution to their victims.  In this case,
insurance companies could guarantee that the victims would be paid if the
convict escaped; they would be responsible for security.  Entrepreneurs
would compete to offer employment to the inmates.  Reasonable room, board
and security expenses would be subtracted from an inmate's earnings, the
remainder going to his victim until the debt had been satisfied, at which
time he's free to go--just not back to any private community that doesn't
accept ex-cons.
 
If the inmate is unwilling to work under the condition that all of his
discretionary income go his victim, perhaps he can negotiate better terms
with said victim.  (I won't work at all, unless I can keep something for
myself.)
 
If the inmate is unwilling (or unable) to work under any conditions, perhaps
a private charity would cover his overhead (presumably at a minimum
subsistance level) until he changes his mind.  If none is willing to do so,
we still have options:  1) Exile him from our community.  2) Cause him harm,
until the loss to him is equal to what he owes.                     
 
One option which appeals to me esthetically is to sell one of his organs.
This provides value to his victim, not merely loss to the criminal.
(Presumably this level of compensation could only be justified to pay for a
particularly heinous crime, even then only if that convict refused to work
off his debt in a more conventional manner.)
 
> I would never trust anyone who murdered a human being.  Never.
 
That's your right, however people DO change.  Personally, I take it on a
case by case basis.  (Not that I hang out with that many murderers)  ;-).
 
 
