From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: Re: PPL: Why Not Let Convicts Run the Police?

Mark Read Pickens writes:
>Someone who didn't sign the agreement, who gained access to this private
>community by trespass, can be assumed to have given implicit consent to
>abide by the terms of that contract.

I don't agree with the notion of implicit consent.  Consider the following
example: a sealed package (containing, typically, computer software) bears
on its outside a licence agreement and a label saying: opening of this
package constitutes agreement to the terms of this licence.  I say "no it
doesn't", and open it anyway.  Whether such shrinkwrap licences are legally
binding under a given legal system is a matter for the relevant courts to
decide.  I am sceptical of whether they should be (i.e. whether it would in
general be beneficial if they were deemed to be binding.

If I know that trespassers may be shot on sight, that may influence my
decision whether to trespass and the preparations I make before doing so. 
It surely does not require that, if caught, I have agreed to be shot and
must therefore meekly let it happen.  It might imply that my own PPL agency
won't defend me -- or it might not.  That depends on whatever rules it
happens to have.

